  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n\n     We will use a multi-resolution optimization approach combined with gradient-based and population-based search. The key idea is that the optimal solution often has certain symmetries or repeating structures at different scales. By gradually increasing grid resolution (from 300 to 600) and reuse of best solutions across resolutions, we can both refine existing candidates and potentially discover new, better solutions. This approach addresses the issue of optimization getting stuck in local minima by leveraging higher resolutions and semi-gradient information. It also benefits from the fact that the current record holder (0.380865) is already quite good, so we focus on incremental improvment via resolution upgrades.\n\n 2. Mathematical specification:\n\n     - Our variables are discrete grid functions h \u2261 h(x) \u2261 h_i for i=0,...,n-1 on [0,2].\n     - Objective: We minimize C5 = max_{k=-n+1}^{n-1} \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n     - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 and sum(h) = n/2 exactly.\n\n     We treat grid upscaling as a continuous function space problem using interpolation. The projected gradient method is used to enforce constraints locally at each grid iteration.\n\n 3. Implementation procedure:\n\n     Let's define helper functions first:\n\n        def linear_interpolate(h_old, n_old, n_new):\n            \"\"\"Interpolate a grid function with n_old points to n_new points\"\"\"\n            x_old = np.linspace(0,2, n_old)\n            x_new = np.linspace(0,2, n_new)\n            return np.interp(x_new, x_old, h_old)\n\n        def projection(h):\n            \"\"\"Ensure 0\u2264h\u22641 and sum(h)=n/2\"\"\"\n            h = h.copy()\n            gap = n/2 - sum(h)\n            if abs(gap) < 1e-14:\n                return h\n            if gap > 0:\n                # If missing mass, add proportionally to highest regions\n                h += gap * h/sum(h)\n            else:\n                h += gap * h/sum(-h)   # Gradient of -sum(h) is cell-based but linear\n            h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n            return h\n\n        def compute_overlap(h, n):\n            \"\"\"Compute the value of C5 for a grid function\"\"\"\n            dx = 2/n\n            c = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n            return np.max(c) * dx\n\n        def optimize_grid(h0, n):\n            \"\"\"Perform local optimization at grid size n\"\"\"\n            res = minimize(objective, h0, method='Nelder-Mead')\n            return projection(res.x)\n\n        def objective(x):\n            \"\"\"The optimization objective function\"\"\"\n            return compute_overlap(x)\n\n        def semi_gradient(h, n):\n            \"\"\"Compute gradient estimate for the max overlap\"\"\"\n            c = correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n            idx = np.argmax(c)\n            c_approx = c * (2.0/n)  # Normalized by grid spacing\n            # Estimate gradient by perturbing two high-contribution points\n            # Using the lag that contributed most to the max\n            lag_idx = idx - (n-1)  # Convert to shift index\n            dx = 2/n\n            grad = np.zeros(n)\n            i1, i2 = ..., ...  # Need to find two points contributing to this lag\n            # This is a simplification - in practice find points where contribution is max\n            grad[i1] = ..., grad[i2] = ...\n            return grad\n\n     The main loop:\n\n        1. Set grid sizes from 300 to 320, 340, ..., 600 with appropriate step sizes.\n        2. Start optimization at first grid size using initial_h_values (if available)\n        3. For subsequent grids:\n            - Interpolate best solution from previous grid and project\n            - Perform partial optimization (e.g. 50 iterations)\n        4. Keep track of best solution (lowest C5) across all grids\n        5. If time remains, try no-gradient steepest descent on current best solution\n\n     4. Search choices and useful variations:\n\n        - Grid progression: Start from parent grid with 300 points, then move to 310, 320,... until 600\n        - Number of optimizer iterations per grid: Starting with 50 for the coarsest, 30 for medium, 10 for fine-grids\n        - Projection regularized to avoid 0/1 boundaries unless forced\n\n     5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\n        - Grid size 300: ~1-2 minutes\n        - Step every 5 points: 5-10 minutes per grid pads\n        - Total budget: ~5-1 minutes for grids up to 600 points\n\n     6. Validation and return contract:\n\n        - Always verify h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) = n/2 after interpolation/projection\n        - Return the best solution (lowest C5) found, along with grid and score\n\n 4. Search choices and useful variations:\n\n    V1 (Base): Optimize progression grids (300,310,...,600)\n    V2 (Resampling): If stuck at local min, resample new points uniformly random on current grid\n    V3 (Gradient-based):\n        Use semi-gradient to guide steepest descent (direction of largest improvement)\n\n 5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\n        for grid in range(initial, final, step):\n            total_time += (grid_size_effort * optimizer_time) + (interpolate_time)\n            if total_time > budget: break\n\n 6. Validation and return contract:\n\n        def validate(h):\n            if not(all(0<=x<=1 for x in h) and abs(sum(h)-n/2)<1e-10:\n                raise ValueError(\"Infeasible solution\")\n            return sum(overlap(h) < target)\n\n        Return: (h, compute_overlap(h), grid_size)",
